'''Thomas Patrick Tiffany''' (born December 30, 1957)<ref>{{cite web |title=Senator Thomas Tiffany |url=https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2019/legislators/senate/1922 |website=Wisconsin State Legislature |access-date=November 5, 2024}}</ref> is an American politician and businessman from Wisconsin. A member of the [[United States House of Representatives]], he has represented [[Wisconsin's 7th congressional district]] since 2020. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he is a member of the House [[Freedom Caucus]].

Tiffany worked in business and as the supervisor of the town of [[Little Rice, Wisconsin|Little Rice]] before his election in 2010 to the [[Wisconsin State Assembly]]. Later elected to the [[Wisconsin State Senate]] in 2012,<ref name="bb2019bio">{{cite book|url= https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/misc/lrb/blue_book/2019_2020/040_state_legislators.pdf|title= Wisconsin Blue Book 2019-2020|publisher= State of Wisconsin|isbn= 978-1-7333817-0-3|location= [[Madison, Wisconsin]]|year=2019|page=56}}</ref> he became the chair of the Senate Committee on Sporting Heritage, Mining and Forestry.<ref name=SHMF>{{cite web|url=http://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2017/committees/senate/1762|title=2017 Senate Committee on Sporting Heritage, Mining and Forestry |website=Wisconsin State Legislature|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181026074905/http://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2017/committees/senate/1762|archive-date=October 26, 2018}}</ref> He was elected to the U.S. House of Representatives following a [[2020 Wisconsin's 7th congressional district special election|special election in 2020]]. Tiffany is the Republican nominee for [[governor of Wisconsin]] in the upcoming [[2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name=":2">{{cite news |last=Andrea |first=Lawrence |title=U.S. Rep. Tom Tiffany enters the 2026 race for Wisconsin governor, 3rd Republican in the field |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2025/09/23/u-s-rep-tom-tiffany-enters-the-2026-race-for-wisconsin-governor/85375533007 |access-date=September 23, 2025 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]|date=September 23, 2025 }}</ref>

==Early life, education, and career==

Tiffany was born in [[Wabasha, Minnesota]], and grew up on a dairy farm near [[Elmwood, Wisconsin]], with five brothers and two sisters.<ref name="bio">{{cite web|url=http://www.tomtiffanyforassembly.com/biography |title= Biography |website= Tom Tiffany for Assembly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100807102243/http://www.tomtiffanyforassembly.com/biography |archive-date=August 7, 2010 |via=the [[Wayback Machine]]}}</ref> He graduated from [[Elmwood High School (Elmwood, Wisconsin)|Elmwood High School]] in 1976 and earned a [[Bachelor of Science]] in [[agricultural economics]] from the [[University of Wisconsin–River Falls]] in 1980.<ref name="bb2019bio"/>

Tiffany managed the petroleum division of High Plains Cooperative<ref>{{cite web|url=https://drydenwire.com/news/15-things-you-might-not-know-about-me-tom-tiffany|title= 15 Things You Might Not Know About Me: Tom Tiffany|date=October 22, 2020|website=Dryden Wire}}</ref> in [[Plainview, Minnesota]], before moving to [[Minocqua, Wisconsin]], to manage Zenker Oil Company's petroleum distribution in 1988.<ref name="antigo2012">{{cite news|url=http://www.antigodailyjournal.com/full.php?id=14497 |title= Tom Tiffany will seek northern Senate post  |date= March 29, 2012 |newspaper= Antigo Daily Journal |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120401181533/http://www.antigodailyjournal.com/full.php?id=14497 |archive-date=April 1, 2012|access-date=May 2, 2020|via=the [[Wayback Machine]]}}</ref> He and his wife, Chris, operated an excursion business, Wilderness Queen Cruise Line, on the [[Willow Flowage]] from 1991 to 2006. They moved the business to the [[Wisconsin River]] in [[Rhinelander, Wisconsin|Rhinelander]] in 2007 before they sold the business in 2012.<ref>{{cite news|title=Sunday, October 5, 1997|newspaper=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]|date=October 5, 1997|quote=Tom Tiffany, manager of both the Cedar Falls campground on the north end of the flowage and the Wilderness Queen cruise line, walks through these bountiful acres every morning}}</ref><ref name="Bergquist2015">{{cite news|last=Bergquist|first=Lee|title=Tom Tiffany has been a lightning rod on environmental issues|work=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel|date=August 6, 2015|url=https://archive.jsonline.com/news/statepolitics/tom-tiffany-has-been-a-lightning-rod-on-environmental-issues-b99547320z1-320987941.html|access-date=March 30, 2026}}</ref>

Tiffany was the supervisor of the town of [[Little Rice, Wisconsin]], from 2009 to 2013, was a dam tender for the [[Wisconsin Valley Improvement Company]],<ref name="bb2019bio"/> and is a former member of the Oneida County Economic Development Corporation.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wpr.org/person/tom-tiffany|title=Tom Tiffany|website=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]]|access-date=August 2, 2026}}</ref>

==Wisconsin Legislature (2011–2020)==
In 2004 and 2008, he ran to represent the [[Wisconsin's 12th Senate district|12th district]] in the [[Wisconsin State Senate]], first against [[Roger Breske]], and then [[Jim Holperin]], losing both times.<ref name="2004DecWEC" /><ref name="2008NovWEC" />

In 2010, Tiffany ran for the [[Wisconsin's 35th Assembly district|35th district]] of the Wisconsin State Assembly after the incumbent, [[Donald Friske]], retired. Tiffany won the primary and defeated Democratic nominee Jay Schmelling, 58.09% to 41.81%.<ref name="antigo2012"/>

In 2012, Tiffany chose not to seek reelection to the Assembly and instead to run again for the state senate after Holperin announced he would not run for reelection. He defeated the Democratic nominee Susan Sommer, 56% to 40%, in the general election.<ref name="antigo2012"/><ref>{{cite news|url=http://badgerherald.com/news/2012/11/07/republicans_take_sta.php |title= Republicans Take State Senate |last=Kirkby|first= Sean |newspaper= [[The Badger Herald]] |date= November 7, 2012 |access-date= May 2, 2020}}</ref>

In the state senate, Tiffany was the chair of the chair of the Senate Committee on Sporting Heritage, Mining and Forestry<ref name="SHMF">{{cite web|url=http://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2017/committees/senate/1762|title=2017 Senate Committee on Sporting Heritage, Mining and Forestry |website=Wisconsin State Legislature|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181026074905/http://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2017/committees/senate/1762|archive-date=October 26, 2018}}</ref> and a member of the [[Wisconsin Legislature Joint Committee on Finance|Joint Finance Committee]].<ref name="Bergquist2015"/> He supported mining operations, advocated more logging, and proposed reduced funding for the [[Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources]].<ref name="Bergquist2015"/><ref name="Bergquist2017">{{cite news|last=Bergquist|first=Lee|title=Bill would end Wisconsin moratorium on sulfide mining, create easier path to future mining|work=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel|date=August 22, 2017|url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/2017/08/22/bill-would-end-wisconsin-moratorium-sulfide-mining-create-easier-path-ease-restrictions-future-minin/587792001|access-date=March 30, 2026}}</ref> He also had a role in transferring powers over to the Wisconsin Legislature from the Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources on issues like operating rules for controversial dams in [[Vilas County, Wisconsin|Vilas County]].<ref name="BergquistDam">{{cite news|last=Bergquist|first=Lee|title=Panel says lawmakers — not regulators — will have final say over dam|work=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel |date = June 13, 2015|url=https://archive.jsonline.com/news/statepolitics/307279301.html|access-date=March 30, 2026}}</ref> Tiffany was proud to earn the enmity of the Wisconsin chapter of the [[League of Conservation Voters]] and called organizations such as the [[United States Environmental Protection Agency|Environmental Protection Agency]] under [[Barack Obama|President Obama]] "radical."<ref name="Bergquist2015"/>

==U.S. House of Representatives (2020–present)==
U.S. representative [[Sean Duffy]] resigned on September 23, 2019, after his youngest daughter was diagnosed with a heart condition. Tiffany announced that he would run in a [[2020 Wisconsin's 7th congressional district special election|special election]] to succeed him. He won the February 18, 2020, Republican primary and defeated [[Wausau, Wisconsin|Wausau]] attorney Tricia Zunker in the May 12, 2020, special election.<ref name="primary2020">{{cite news|url=https://www.wausaudailyherald.com/story/news/2020/02/18/tricia-zunker-and-tom-tiffany-advance-may-special-election-7th-congressional-district/4759598002|title=Tricia Zunker and Tom Tiffany advance to May special election for Wisconsin 7th Congressional District|date= February 18, 2020|access-date=May 2, 2020 |newspaper=[[Wausau Daily Herald]]|last=Schulte |first=Laura}}</ref> Tiffany was sworn in on May 19, 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Poltrock |first1=Heather |title=Tom Tiffany sworn in to U.S. House of Representatives |url=https://www.wsaw.com/fox/content/news/Congressman-elect-Tom-Tiffany-to-be-sworn-in-Tuesday-570589881.html |publisher=WSAW |date=May 19, 2020 |access-date=May 19, 2020 |archive-date=May 26, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200526105343/https://www.wsaw.com/fox/content/news/Congressman-elect-Tom-Tiffany-to-be-sworn-in-Tuesday-570589881.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> 

Tiffany defeated Zunker in a rematch in the November 3, 2020, general election taking in 60.7% of the vote.<ref name="WIgenr" />

In June 2021, Tiffany was one of 14 House Republicans to vote against legislation to establish June 19, or [[Juneteenth]], as a [[federal holiday]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/16/politics/house-vote-juneteenth-federal-holiday-senate-passed-june-19/index.html|title=Congress passes bill making Juneteenth a federal holiday|publisher=CNN|last1=Grayer|first1=Annie|last2=Diaz|first2=Danielle|date=June 16, 2021|access-date=June 16, 2021|archive-date=June 16, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210616160046/https://www.cnn.com/2021/06/16/politics/house-vote-juneteenth-federal-holiday-senate-passed-june-19/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In early 2026, Tiffany introduced legislation to raise the status of [[Apostle Islands National Lakeshore]] into a [[List of national parks of the United States|national park]].<ref name="HR5497">{{Cite web |title=H.R. 5497 — Apostle Islands National Park and Preserve Act |url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/5497 |access-date=March 19, 2026 |website=[[Congress.gov]] |date=February 11, 2026 }}</ref>

