==2026 gubernatorial campaign==
{{main|2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election}}
[[File:Tom Tiffany - 55022139480.jpg|thumb|Tiffany speaking at the 2025 [[AmericaFest]] convention]]

On September 23, 2025, Tiffany announced his campaign for governor of Wisconsin in the [[2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref name=":2" /> 

He was endorsed by [[Donald Trump]] in January 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 28, 2026 |title=Trump endorses Republican Rep. Tom Tiffany in Wisconsin's open race for governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/trump-tom-tiffany-endorsement-wisconsin-governor-ba00045a282245436b822656fc80e6a7 |access-date=January 28, 2026 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> The next day, his main opponent in the primary dropped out of the race.<ref>{{cite web |date=January 30, 2026 |title=How Trump's Tom Tiffany endorsement scrambles Wisconsin governor race |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2026/01/30/how-trumps-tom-tiffany-endorsement-scrambles-wisconsin-governor-race/88414093007/ |access-date=July 21, 2026 |website=WisPolitics}}</ref> All of Tiffany's Republican colleagues from Wisconsin in the U.S. House of Representatives have endorsed him.<ref>{{cite web |date=September 30, 2025 |title=Tiffany campaign: Congressman Scott Fitzgerald endorses Tom Tiffany's campaign for governor |url=https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-campaign-congressman-scott-fitzgerald-endorses-tom-tiffanys-campaign-for-governor/ |access-date=September 30, 2025 |website=WisPolitics}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=October 3, 2025 |title=Tiffany campaign: Congressman Glenn Grothman officially endorses Tiffany's run for governor|url=https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-campaign-congressman-glenn-grothman-officially-endorses-tiffanys-run-for-governor/|access-date=October 3, 2025 |website=WisPolitics}}</ref><ref name=SteilJan>{{cite web |date=December 19, 2025 |title=Tiffany campaign: Congressman Bryan Steil endorses Tom Tiffany's gubernatorial run
|url=https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-campaign-congressman-bryan-steil-endorses-tom-tiffanys-gubernatorial-run/|access-date=December 22, 2025 |website=WisPolitics}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=October 16, 2025 |title=Tiffany campaign: Tiffany campaign: Congressman Derrick Van Orden endorses Tom Tiffany's gubernatorial run |url=https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-campaign-congressman-derrick-van-orden-endorses-tom-tiffanys-gubernatorial-run/|access-date=October 18, 2025 |website=WisPolitics}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=September 25, 2025 |title=Tiffany campaign: Congressman Tony Wied formally endorses Tom Tiffany for governor |url=https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-campaign-congressman-tony-wied-formally-endorses-tom-tiffany-for-governor/ |access-date=September 25, 2025 |website=WisPolitics}}</ref> Republican strategists have said this allowed Tiffany to avoid rebuilding the party after the primary, noting that the election is for "only the second open gubernatorial seat in the last 60 years in Wisconsin, and there won't be a primary on the Republican side."<ref>{{cite web|last=Opoien|first=Jessie|date=January 30, 2026 |title=How Trump's Tom Tiffany endorsement scrambles Wisconsin governor race |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2026/01/30/how-trumps-tom-tiffany-endorsement-scrambles-wisconsin-governor-race/88414093007|access-date=July 21, 2026|website=WisPolitics}}</ref>

In 2026, Tiffany, while campaigning for governor, commented on the [[Menomonee Falls]] high school starting a black students' club, by stating that "Race-based preferences are un-American, illegal, and must end." Republican candidate for lieutenant governor Will Martin, an African-American whose mother, Tanya Martin, helped integrate his school in their Tennessee hometown,  commented that student clubs must be open to all without discrimination or exclusion.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bentley|first=Drake |date=May 26, 2026|title=Black Republican candidate for Wisconsin Lt. Gov. remarks on Menomonee Falls Black student club |url=https://milwaukeecourier.com/news/2026/05/26/black-republican-candidate-for-wisconsin-lt-gov-remarks-on-menomonee-falls-black-student-club|work=[[The Milwaukee Courier]] |access-date=August 9, 2026|ref=none}}</ref>

In July 2026, Tiffany said most election clerks around Wisconsin do good work, "but there's a couple clerks that have fallen down on the job, and we've got to make sure we have election integrity."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kremer|first=Rich|date=July 20, 2026 |title=As the Democratic primary heats up, Republican Tom Tiffany has campaigned for governor on his own terms |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/republican-tom-tiffany-campaign-wisconsin-governor-primary-2026 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260720163144/https://www.wpr.org/news/republican-tom-tiffany-campaign-wisconsin-governor-primary-2026 |archive-date=July 20, 2026 |access-date=July 20, 2026 |website=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]]}}</ref>

In August 2026, Tiffany proposed to have the [[Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources]] split, in order to "separate fish, wildlife, hunting, fishing, trapping from the environmental permitting part of it," saying that this would benefit the state's sportsmen.<ref>{{cite news |last= Bentley |first=Drake |date=August 4, 2026 |title=Tom Tiffany pushes extreme Wisconsin DNR break up plan opposed by Republican officials |url=https://milwaukeecourier.com/news/2026/08/04/tom-tiffany-pushes-extreme-wisconsin-dnr-break-up-plan-opposed-by-republican-officials |work=The Milwaukee Courier |access-date=August 10, 2026}}</ref>

In September 2026, Tiffany was flying back from a campaign event when the light aircraft he was flying in lost power. The plane made an emergency landing on a lake; as the plane began to take on water, Tiffany and the pilot swam to safety. Tiffany received stitches on his face prior to being released from the hospital.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sands |first1=Leo |title=Tom Tiffany, Wisconsin Representative, Sustains Minor Injuries After Plane Lands in Lake |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/13/us/politics/tom-tiffany-wisconsin-lake-wausau-plane-crash.html |access-date=14 September 2026 |work=The New York Times |date=13 September 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Rep. Tom Tiffany says he sustained minor injuries in emergency plane landing on lake |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/wisconsin-tom-tiffany-sustained-minor-injuries-plane-emergency-lake-rcna597456 |access-date=14 September 2026 |work=NBC News |date=13 September 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

==Political positions==

===2020 United States presidential election===
In his first year in Congress, Tiffany supported [[Donald Trump]]'s [[attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election]]. In December 2020, Tiffany was one of 126 Republican members of the U.S. House of Representatives to sign an [[amicus brief]] in support of ''[[Texas v. Pennsylvania]]'', a lawsuit filed at the [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]] contesting the results of the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]], in which [[Joe Biden]] defeated<ref>{{cite web|last1=Blood|first1=Michael R.|last2=Riccardi|first2=Nicholas|date=December 5, 2020|title=Biden officially secures enough electors to become president|url=https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-joe-biden-donald-trump-elections-electoral-college-3e0b852c3cfadf853b08aecbfc3569fa|url-status=live|access-date=December 12, 2020|website=[[Associated Press|AP News]]|archive-date=December 8, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201208201209/https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-joe-biden-donald-trump-elections-electoral-college-3e0b852c3cfadf853b08aecbfc3569fa}}</ref> the incumbent president [[Donald Trump]]. The Supreme Court declined to hear the case on the basis that Texas lacked [[Standing (law)|standing]] under [[Article Three of the United States Constitution|Article III of the Constitution]] to challenge the results of an election held by another state.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Liptak|first=Adam|author-link=Adam Liptak|date=December 11, 2020|title=Supreme Court Rejects Texas Suit Seeking to Subvert Election|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/11/us/politics/supreme-court-election-texas.html|access-date=December 12, 2020|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=December 11, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201211234955/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/12/11/us/politics/supreme-court-election-texas.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":1">{{cite web|title=Order in Pending Case|url=https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/121120zr_p860.pdf|date=December 11, 2020|publisher=[[Supreme Court of the United States]]|access-date=December 11, 2020|archive-date=December 11, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201211234004/https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/121120zr_p860.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/12/10/politics/read-house-republicans-texas-supreme-court/index.html|title=Brief from 126 Republicans supporting Texas lawsuit in Supreme Court|first=Daniella |last=Diaz|work=[[CNN]]|access-date=December 11, 2020|archive-date=December 12, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201212000435/https://www.cnn.com/2020/12/10/politics/read-house-republicans-texas-supreme-court/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Tiffany was also among the 120 House members, all [[Republican Party (United States)|Republicans]], who objected to counting Arizona's and Pennsylvania's [[United States Electoral College|electoral votes]] in the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jan/07/list-republicans-voted-to-reject-election-results |title=The long list of Republicans who voted to reject election results |last=Chang |first=Alvin |date=January 7, 2021 |work=The Guardian |access-date=January 8, 2021 |archive-date=May 17, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210517192023/https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jan/07/list-republicans-voted-to-reject-election-results |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://apnews.com/article/joe-biden-donald-trump-wisconsin-elections-electoral-college-0657e37b099f9efaf7cfd8ce1bbe31de |title=GOP Reps. Tiffany, Fitzgerald object to certifying Biden win |last=Bauer |first=Scott |date=January 7, 2021 |website=AP News |publisher=Associated Press |access-date=January 8, 2021}}</ref>

Tiffany supports ongoing efforts to revisit the [[2020 United States presidential election| 2020 election]] and has supported previous efforts to overturn it.<ref name="2020ElectionMJS20260519">{{Cite web |last1=Beck |first1=Molly |last2=Karnopp |first2=Hope |date=May 19, 2026 |title=As the Democratic primary heats up, Republican Tom Tiffany has campaigned for governor on his own terms |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2026/05/19/tiffany-backs-fbi-probe-into-2020-vote-wont-say-whether-biden-won/90141082007/ |access-date=July 30, 2026 |website=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]}}</ref> He has said he would have rejected the 2020 vote for Biden in Wisconsin if it had been up to the legislature, and he supported a Texas lawsuit to overturn the 2020 election in various other states.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Gilbert |first1=Craig |last2=Glauber |first2=Bill |date=January 6, 2021 |title=Tom Tiffany, Scott Fitzgerald say they would have rejected Biden's victory in Wisconsin had there been a vote in Congress|url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2021/01/06/baldwin-johnson-vote-against-objecting-electoral-college-vote/6576579002|access-date=July 30, 2026 |website=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]}}</ref><ref name="2020ElectionMJS20260519" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Glauber |first=Bill |date=December 10, 2020 |title=Tom Tiffany among 106 House Republicans backing Texas lawsuit to overturn election|url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/12/10/tom-tiffany-backs-texas-lawsuit-overturn-election/3886417001 |access-date=July 30, 2026 |website=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]}}</ref>

===Abortion===

In September 2025, Tiffany declined to say whether he supported a heartbeat abortion ban such as one he supported during his time in the state legislature.<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 25, 2025 |title=Tiffany declines to say if he still backs 6-week abortion ban |url=https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-declines-to-say-if-he-still-backs-6-week-abortion-ban |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260605060216/https://www.wispolitics.com/2025/tiffany-declines-to-say-if-he-still-backs-6-week-abortion-ban|archive-date=June 5, 2026 |access-date=July 8, 2026 |website=[[Wispolitics|WISPOLITICS]]}}</ref> In January 2026, [[PBS]] asked him "Would you like to see any changes to Wisconsin's abortion law?" and he answered "No. So, I voted for the current law, which, 10 years ago when I was in the state legislature, which is you can have an abortion up to five months. I voted for that law, and as governor I will uphold it."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schultz |first=Zac |date=January 7, 2026 |title=Tom Tiffany on issues in Wisconsin's 2026 governor's race |url=https://pbswisconsin.org/news-item/tom-tiffany-on-issues-in-wisconsins-2026-governors-race/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260124050050/https://pbswisconsin.org/news-item/tom-tiffany-on-issues-in-wisconsins-2026-governors-race/ |archive-date=January 24, 2026 |access-date=July 8, 2026 |website=[[PBS Wisconsin]]}}</ref>

===Foreign policy===

====China and Taiwan====

Tiffany has expressed support for recognition of [[Taiwan]] as an independent state. In 2023, Tiffany authored an op-ed in ''[[The Washington Times]]'', stating that "the United States should lead by example and end this tired charade."<ref>{{cite web |date=December 21, 2023 |title=2023-1221: U.S. Must End Its "One China" Policy: Rep. Tom Tiffany; Taiwan Retains No.1 in Asia on Freedom Index; U.S. Arms Sales to Taiwan |url=https://fapa.org/2023-1221-u-s-must-end-its-one-china-policy-rep-tom-tiffany-taiwan-retains-no-1-in-asia-on-freedom-index-u-s-arms-sales-to-taiwan |access-date=December 5, 2024 |website=Formosan Association for Public Affairs}}</ref> Since being elected to the House, Tiffany has introduced legislation in every session to establish diplomatic relations with Taiwan.<ref>{{cite web |title=H. CON. RES. |url=https://tiffany.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/tiffany.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/taiwan_resolution_2023.pdf |access-date=December 5, 2024 |website=Congress.gov |publisher=United States House of Representatives}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=H. CON. RES. 21 |url=https://www.congress.gov/117/bills/hconres21/BILLS-117hconres21ih.pdf |access-date=December 5, 2024 |website=Congress.gov |publisher=United States House of Representatives}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=H. CON. RES. |url=https://tiffany.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/tiffany.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/Taiwan_Resolution.pdf |access-date=February 24, 2025 |website=Congress.gov |publisher=United States House of Representatives}}</ref> In 2024, Tiffany introduced legislation expressing support for Taiwan's full participation at the [[World Health Organization]], as well as legislation restricting the [[United States Department of State|U.S. Department of State]] from using funds to enforce restrictions on "high-level communications" with Taiwanese officials. Both pieces of legislation were passed by Congress.<ref>{{cite web |date=September 14, 2024 |title=US House passes Taiwan WHO participation bill |url=https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/front/archives/2024/09/14/2003823758 |access-date=January 4, 2025 |website=Taipei Times}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Everington |first1=Keoni |date=June 28, 2024 |title=Congress approves amendment lifting limits between US, Taiwan officials |url=https://www.taiwannews.com.tw/news/5896780 |access-date=January 4, 2025 |website=Taiwan News}}</ref>

====Russo-Ukrainian war====

In May 2022, Tiffany "strongly" condemned the [[2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine]], but did not support a bill for almost $40 billion in additional American funding for Ukraine, stating that American taxpayers should not be financing "an open-ended European conflict."<ref>{{cite web |publisher=House of Representatives |date=May 10, 2022|website=Office of Tom Tiffany|url=https://tiffany.house.gov/media/press-releases/tiffany-statement-supplemental-funding-ukraine |title=Tiffany Statement on Supplemental Funding for Ukraine |access-date=August 10, 2026}}</ref> 

On March 19, 2024, Tiffany was one of nine Republicans to vote against a resolution condemning the [[Child abductions in the Russo-Ukrainian war|abduction of Ukrainian children into Russia]] during the [[Russo-Ukrainian war]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Metzger |first1=Bryan |date=March 19, 2024 |title=These 9 House Republicans voted against a resolution condemning the Russian abduction of Ukrainian children |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/house-republicans-vote-against-condemning-russian-abduction-ukrainian-children-2024-3 |website=Business Insider|access-date=August 7, 2026}}</ref>

==== Israel ====

After the [[October 7 attacks]], Tiffany said he firmly supported Israel and would not support a ceasefire until "Hamas has been neutralized by Israel".<ref name=":4">[https://www.wxpr.org/politics-government/2024-03-05/tiffany-supportive-of-israel-despite-calls-for-a-ceasefire ''Tiffany supportive of Israel despite calls for a ceasefire''], WXPR, March 5, 2024 </ref> In 2023, he voted in favor of a Republican-led House bill that would provide $14 billion in aid to Israel.<ref name=":4" />

When asked why he supports foreign aid to Israel but not Ukraine, Tiffany said he trusted Israel to responsibly use aid but he does not feel the same way about Ukraine.<ref>''[https://www.cbs58.com/news/wi-members-of-congress-split-over-israel-aid-whether-us-should-back-ceasefire WI members of Congress split over Israel aid, whether U.S. should back ceasefire]'', Nov 1, 2023, CBS</ref>

====War powers====
In June 2021, Tiffany was one of 49 House Republicans who voted to repeal the [[Authorization for Use of Military Force Against Iraq Resolution of 2002]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 17, 2021 |title=House votes to repeal 2002 Iraq War authorization |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/house-set-repeal-2002-iraq-war-authorization-n1271107 |website=[[NBC News]] |access-date=June 17, 2021 |archive-date=June 18, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618123910/https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/house-set-repeal-2002-iraq-war-authorization-n1271107 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Final vote results for roll call 172 |url=https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2021/roll172.xml |access-date=December 2, 2023 |website=clerk.house.gov|archive-date=June 27, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210627182635/https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2021/roll172.xml|url-status=live}}</ref>

In March 2023, Tiffany was among 47 Republicans to vote in favor of House Congressional Resolution 21, which directed President [[Joe Biden]] to remove U.S. troops from [[Syria]] within 180 days.<ref>{{cite web |date=March 8, 2023 |title=H.Con.Res. 21: Directing the President, pursuant to section 5(c) of ... -- House Vote #136 -- Mar 8, 2023 |url=https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/118-2023/h136 |access-date=March 10, 2023 |archive-date=September 19, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240919032327/https://www.govtrack.us/congress/votes/118-2023/h136 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=March 8, 2023 |title=House Votes Down Bill Directing Removal of Troops From Syria |url=https://www.usnews.com/news/politics/articles/2023-03-08/house-votes-down-bill-directing-removal-of-troops-from-syria |agency=Associated Press |archive-date=April 4, 2023 |access-date=March 10, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404204512/https://www.usnews.com/news/politics/articles/2023-03-08/house-votes-down-bill-directing-removal-of-troops-from-syria|url-status=live}}</ref> In June, Tiffany was among 71 members who voted against final passage of the [[Fiscal Responsibility Act of 2023]] in the House.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gans |first=Jared |date=May 31, 2023 |title=Republicans and Democrats who bucked party leaders by voting no |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4029522-republicans-and-democrats-who-bucked-party-leaders-by-voting-no |access-date=June 6, 2023 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |archive-date=June 1, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230601031919/https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4029522-republicans-and-democrats-who-bucked-party-leaders-by-voting-no|url-status=live }}</ref> Tiffany has supported some efforts for infrastructure in his district. In September 2023, he joined a bipartisan group of legislators to request a replacement for the [[John A. Blatnik Bridge|Blatnik Bridge]] connecting [[Duluth, Minnesota]] and [[Superior, Wisconsin]].<ref name="BlatnikLetter2023">{{cite web |last=Klobuchar |first=Amy |author-link=Amy Klobuchar |display-authors=et al. |date=September 27, 2023 |title=Letter to President Biden Regarding Blatnik Bridge Replacement |url=https://stauber.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/stauber.house.gov/files/evo-media-document/letter-to-president-biden-re-blatnik-bridge-final.pdf |access-date=March 19, 2026 |publisher=U.S. House of Representatives}}</ref> The [[United States Department of Transportation|U.S. Department of Transportation]] under Joe Biden granted $1 billion to this project.<ref>{{Cite news |date=January 22, 2024 |title=More than $1 billion awarded to Minnesota, Wisconsin bridge |url=https://apnews.com/article/blatnik-bridge-minnesota-wisconsin-duluth-superior-25577cff694848f4c711acaa3e1a5ece |access-date=January 23, 2024 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref>

In May 2026, Tiffany voted against an Iran [[war powers resolution]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Karnopp |first=Hope |date=May 18, 2026 |others=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel |title=Tom Tiffany's Iran war vote contradicts his own podcast comments |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/05/18/tom-tiffany-says-time-to-wrap-up-iran-conflict-but-votes-no-on-ending-it/90133522007/?gnt-cfr=1&gca-cat=p |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=USA Today}}</ref> The resolution was intended to limit the [[2026 Iran war]], directing President Trump to remove the U.S. military from the conflict, until congressional consent had been obtained.<ref name=":3"/> Tiffany said about his vote, "I think most Americans agree that it's reasonable to want things to start winding down in Iran, while also recognizing that it is unreasonable to undermine the president's role as [[commander-in-chief]]."<ref name=":3"/>

===Tariffs===
In January 2025, at a listening session in [[Rice Lake, Wisconsin]], Tiffany spoke in approval of [[tariffs in the second Trump administration]], saying that they are "very strategic, very targeted and ultimately they are meant to benefit the American people".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://fox99.com/tiffany-says-trump-tariffs-are-strategic-negotiation-tactic|title=Tiffany says Trump tariffs are strategic negotiation tactic|publisher=Fox 99|date=January 28, 2025}}</ref> In 2025, Tiffany stated "we clearly have not had [[fair trade]] here in America."<ref>{{cite news |last=van Wagtendonk |first=Anya |date=April 3, 2025 |title=Wisconsin leaders divide by party over support for Trump tariffs|url=https://www.wpr.org/news/wisconsin-trump-tariffs-johnson-baldwin-economic-development|work=Wisconsin Public Radio |access-date=August 10, 2026}}</ref>

==Personal life==

Tiffany and his wife, Christine, have three children.<ref name="bio"/> The Tiffanys are [[Protestantism in the United States|Protestant]].<ref>{{Cite report |url=https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/wp-content/uploads/sites/7/2022/12/PF_2023.01.03_congress_LIST.pdf |title=Religious affiliation of members of 118th Congress |date=January 3, 2023 |publisher=[[Pew Research Center]] |access-date=April 8, 2023 |archive-date=March 16, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230316090407/https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/wp-content/uploads/sites/7/2022/12/PF_2023.01.03_congress_LIST.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref>

